#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# re

#% text
# `re(z)` gives the real part of the complex number `z`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Exact, machine and ball numbers give their real part; a real number is its own real part.

#% item
# Closed numeric expressions whose value the system knows in exact form, such as `exp(im*pi/3)`, give an exact real part.

#% item
# A symbol stays inside `re`, since it may be complex.

#% item
# `re` is listable: it applies to each element of a list.

#% item
# The imaginary part is `im_part(z)`. The name `im` is the imaginary unit, not a function.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
re(3 + 4im)
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
re(1.0 + 2.0*im)
#% output
#= 1.0

#% text
# A real number is its own real part:

#% cell
re(-7)
#% output
#= -7

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# An exact complex fraction:

#% cell
re(1/2 + 3im/4)
#% output
#= 1/2

#% text
# Closed numeric expressions:

#% cell
re(exp(im*pi/3))
#% output
#= 1/2

#% cell
re(log(-1))
#% output
#= 0

#% text
# A ball:

#% cell
re(n(exp(im), 20))
#% output
#= 0.540302305868139717400937

#% text
# Each element of a list:

#% cell
re([1 + im, 2 - 3im])
#% output
#= [1, 2]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The real and imaginary parts:

#% cell
[re(3 + 4im), im_part(3 + 4im)]
#% output
#= [3, 4]

#% text
# Conjugation does not change the real part:

#% cell
re(conjugate(z))
#% output
#= re(z)

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A symbol may be complex, so its real part stays:

#% cell
re(x + im*y)
#% output
#= re(x + im*y)

#% text
# Closed forms without a known exact value stay; use `n` for a value:

#% cell
re(exp(1 + im))
#% output
#= re(exp(1 + im))

#% cell
n(re(exp(1 + im)))
#% output
#= 1.4686939399158851

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `im_part`, `abs`, `arg`, `conjugate`
